  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1259 AM CDT Tue Jul 14 2026\n\nThe potential for locally heavy rainfall and flash flooding will \ncontinue on Wednesday and Thursday. An upper level low is forecast \nto track across our area on Wednesday and then slide southwestward \ninto the Big Bend region on Thursday. Meanwhile, a very moist \nairmass will remain in place, with precipitable water values of 1.5 \nto 2+ inches across west central Texas. Given the track of the low \ncould see more organized convection develop on Wednesday, especially \nacross our southern/southwestern counties. Nevertheless, given the \nfavorable lift and moist airmass these storms will be very efficient \nrainfall producers. By Friday afternoon the focus for heaviest \nrainfall will begin to shift to our west as the more favorable \nlift exits our area. Ensemble means continue to ping the highest \nrainfall totals through Friday across portions of the northern \nEdwards Plateau into the northwest Hill Country. High pressure \nbegins to build back into the region over the weekend and into \nearly next week. This will result in mainly dry conditions and \ntemperatures climbing back into the mid to upper 90s by Monday.",
